# deadcode baseline for the `api` Go module (PRD #103 M4).
#
# 🔴 THIS FILE IS EMPTY OF ENTRIES AND THAT IS THE DESIGN, NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
# `deadcode -test ./...` reported exactly ONE finding on this module when M4
# landed (2026-08-02, measured at v0.38.0 and v0.48.0 by three parties, all
# agreeing): `HookManager.SettingsPath`, a one-line exported wrapper over the
# unexported `settingsPath()` in the same file, with zero callers. M4 DELETED it
# rather than baselining it, so the gate holds this module at ZERO. That is
# strictly stronger than gate-on-new, and it removes the baseline-rot problem
# instead of managing it.
#
# ADDING A LINE HERE IS A DELIBERATE SUPPRESSION AND OWES A REASON IN A COMMENT
# DIRECTLY ABOVE IT. The routine fix for a deadcode finding is to DELETE the
# function. Two known cases where a suppression is legitimate:
#   - `//go:linkname`: deadcode does not understand it (its own doc says so), so
#     a function reached only that way is a false positive.
#   - a platform-gated file (see the portability note below).
# `scripts/deadcode-gate.sh` treats an entry that is NO LONGER REPORTED as a
# failure, so a suppression cannot outlive the finding it covers.
#
# 🔴 AND THE WEAKER SIBLING HAS NO CI ANSWER, exactly as `.golangci.yml` and both
# `.oxlintrc.json` files record for themselves. That stale-entry rule is what
# stops a suppression rotting; it is NOT what stops one being added. A branch
# author can baseline a genuinely dead function here, in this same file, and the
# pipeline stays green because the gate finds no ADDITION — which is the whole
# design. The comment-with-a-reason convention above is a review aid, not an
# enforced one: nothing in CI reads it. CODEOWNERS on the seven gate-config files
# (both baselines, `.golangci.yml`, both `.oxlintrc.json`, both `knip.jsonc`) is
# the only structural fix, and THERE IS NO CODEOWNERS FILE ANYWHERE IN THIS TREE
# TODAY, so the residual is open for all seven. Recorded in the MR, not closed
# here.
#
# FORMAT: one entry per line, TAB-separated:
#     <package import path>\t<function name>
# Position-free ON PURPOSE (PRD #103 Decision 11). deadcode's default output is
# `path:line:col: unreachable func: Name`, and one inserted comment line above a
# baselined symbol moves it -- so a baseline built from raw output reports a
# spurious addition AND a spurious removal on any unrelated edit. Comment lines
# (`#`) and blank lines are stripped before comparison; no import path and no Go
# identifier can contain `#`.
#
# REGENERATE WITH (by hand -- deliberately NOT a Taskfile target, because a
# fixing variant of a gate is a target testers are told to refuse):
#     ./scripts/deadcode-gate.sh api golang.org/x/tools/cmd/deadcode@v0.48.0 --write
# It re-runs the same producer the gate uses and keeps this header, so the
# regeneration command cannot drift from the checking command.
#
# PORTABILITY: laptop and CI agree today. darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 give
# byte-identical results for this module (measured 2026-08-02). THAT IS A
# PROPERTY OF THIS REPO, NOT OF THE TOOL -- deadcode's own doc says the analysis
# is valid for a single GOOS/GOARCH/-tags configuration, and it holds here only
# because no non-test `.go` file in either Go module carries a `//go:build`
# constraint. Add one and this stops holding; the entry it produces would be
# platform-specific and the baseline would differ between laptop and CI.
